Reasoning: 502 with healthy targets and backend logging 200 means the proxy is rejecting the upstream response, not the app. The checkout path sets a large session JWT, so the upstream response headers likely overflow nginx's proxy buffer. Confirm targets really are healthy first so I don't chase the app.
<|call:check_lb_target_health|> {"target_group": "api-gateway-tg", "include": "state"}
System Response: 6/6 targets healthy, last health check 200 OK, 0 unhealthy -- group is green
Recovery reasoning: Targets are green, so the 502 is generated by nginx between upstream and ALB; the green TG is the trap. Pull ingress-controller error logs scoped to the checkout upstream to see the real proxy error.
<|call:query_access_logs|> {"source": "ingress-controller", "filter": "status=502 path=/checkout/session", "fields": "upstream_status,error"}
System Response: upstream_status=200 but proxy error "upstream sent too big header while reading response header from upstream"
Recovery reasoning: Confirmed -- backend returns 200 with an oversized Set-Cookie/JWT that overflows the default 4k proxy buffer, so nginx converts it to 502. Raise proxy-buffer-size on this ingress only; no app change needed.
<|call:apply_k8s_manifest|> {"kind": "Ingress", "name": "api-gateway", "patch": {"metadata": {"annotations": {"nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/proxy-buffer-size": "16k"}}}}
System Response: ingress updated; controller reloaded; 502 rate on /checkout/session dropped to 0% over the next 5 min
Resolution: Root cause was oversized upstream response headers overflowing nginx's 4k proxy buffer (not the app, which returned 200); fix raised proxy-buffer-size to 16k, leaving the backend response untouched.

Reasoning: 504 only on the large export and only since the data grew points to a request that now runs longer than a fixed timeout in the chain. Reproduce and time it to see whether the LB or the ingress is cutting it off.
<|call:curl_endpoint|> {"url": "https://finance-api.internal/reports/export?range=quarter", "timeout_s": 120, "trace_timing": true}
System Response: HTTP 504 after exactly 60.0s; backend still processing, no upstream response received
Recovery reasoning: The clean 60.0s cutoff is the signature of a fixed read timeout, not the app failing. Default nginx-ingress proxy-read-timeout is 60s while the export now needs more. Confirm the backend actually finishes if given time.
<|call:get_backend_response_time|> {"service": "finance-api", "endpoint": "/reports/export", "range": "quarter"}
System Response: backend completes in 73.4s with 200 OK when queried directly, bypassing the ingress
Recovery reasoning: Backend is healthy but slow; the ingress read timeout is the cap. Raise proxy-read/send-timeout above the worst-case export time on this route only, not globally.
<|call:apply_k8s_manifest|> {"kind": "Ingress", "name": "finance-api", "patch": {"metadata": {"annotations": {"nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/proxy-read-timeout": "120", "nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/proxy-send-timeout": "120"}}}}
System Response: ingress reloaded; /reports/export?range=quarter now returns 200 in 73s; 504 cleared
Resolution: Root cause was the default 60s ingress read timeout being shorter than the grown 73s export; fix raised proxy-read/send-timeout to 120s on just that ingress, leaving other routes at the safer default.

Reasoning: Flapping in lockstep with the load peak and no crashes smells like the health check itself timing out under load rather than the app dying. Snapshot the target group state first, though it may catch a healthy window.
<|call:check_lb_target_health|> {"target_group": "auth-service-tg", "include": "state,reason"}
System Response: 3/3 targets healthy right now, reason "" -- group looks green
Recovery reasoning: Caught it green, which is the trap; a point-in-time check hides the flap. Pull the probe config to see why it oscillates.
<|call:describe_target_group|> {"target_group": "auth-service-tg", "fields": "health_check_path,timeout,interval,healthy_threshold"}
System Response: path=/healthz timeout=2s interval=10s healthy_threshold=2; /healthz runs a synchronous DB ping
Recovery reasoning: /healthz does a synchronous DB ping; under login load it may exceed the 2s probe timeout. Confirm the actual probe latency from the pods before changing anything.
<|call:get_pod_logs|> {"service": "auth-service", "since": "20m", "filter": "healthz"}
System Response: liveness logs OK but /healthz db_ping spikes to 2100ms during peak, over the 2s probe timeout -> intermittent probe failures
Recovery reasoning: The deep DB readiness check exceeds the 2s timeout under load, flapping the targets. Point the health check at a cheap liveness endpoint and widen the timeout instead of touching the app's readiness logic.
<|call:update_health_check|> {"target_group": "auth-service-tg", "path": "/livez", "timeout": 5, "interval": 15, "unhealthy_threshold": 3}
System Response: health check moved to /livez; targets stable healthy for 15 min, no further flaps or 503s
Resolution: Root cause was the health check probing a DB-heavy /healthz with a 2s timeout that failed under login-peak latency; fix moved the probe to a lightweight /livez with a 5s timeout, keeping the deep readiness check for the app's own gating.

Reasoning: A hot single replica with stickiness on and long-lived sockets suggests session pinning. Start with the load balancer's overall health to rule out a broad fault.
<|call:get_lb_metrics|> {"load_balancer": "websocket-chat-alb", "metric": "healthy_host_count,target_response_time", "window": "15m"}
System Response: healthy_host_count=3/3, avg target_response_time=42ms, 2xx rate 99.9% -- LB looks perfectly healthy
Recovery reasoning: Aggregate metrics look fine, which masks the imbalance -- that's the trap. Break it down per target to expose the skew.
<|call:get_lb_metrics|> {"target_group": "websocket-chat-tg", "metric": "request_count_per_target", "window": "15m"}
System Response: target-a 8,900 req | target-b 410 req | target-c 380 req -- heavily skewed to target-a
Recovery reasoning: Distribution confirms pinning, not a metrics artifact. Inspect the stickiness config to see whether it's duration-based and how long the cookie lives.
<|call:get_target_group_attributes|> {"target_group": "websocket-chat-tg", "fields": "stickiness.enabled,stickiness.type,stickiness.duration_seconds,load_balancing_algorithm"}
System Response: stickiness.enabled=true type=lb_cookie duration=86400 algorithm=round_robin
Recovery reasoning: A 24h lb_cookie plus long-lived websockets re-pins every reconnect to target-a. Switch to least-outstanding-requests and shrink the cookie so new/reconnecting sessions spread, without force-closing current sockets.
<|call:modify_lb_attribute|> {"target_group": "websocket-chat-tg", "attributes": {"load_balancing_algorithm": "least_outstanding_requests", "stickiness.duration_seconds": 600}}
System Response: attributes applied; over 10 min target-a CPU 95%->48%, target-b/c rising; live chats stayed connected
Resolution: Root cause was a 24h lb_cookie stickiness pinning reconnects to one replica; fix switched to least-outstanding-requests and a 600s cookie so new sessions rebalance, preserving existing live connections.

Reasoning: Rare 502s with no app error log, clustering in low traffic, is the classic LB-reuses-a-dead-keepalive-connection race. First confirm the app truly logs nothing for these.
<|call:query_access_logs|> {"service": "payments-api", "filter": "status=502", "window": "24h", "fields": "upstream_status,duration"}
System Response: app access log has 0 entries with status=502; every logged request is 2xx/4xx -- the app never saw them
Recovery reasoning: The app never received these requests, so the 502 is born at the LB reusing a connection the backend already closed. Compare the ALB idle timeout to the backend keepalive timeout.
<|call:get_keepalive_settings|> {"targets": ["alb:payments-alb", "backend:payments-api"]}
System Response: alb idle_timeout=60s; backend gunicorn keepalive=5s; backend closes idle conns at 5s
Recovery reasoning: ALB holds connections up to 60s but the backend drops them at 5s, so in quiet periods the ALB sends on a half-closed socket and gets a reset -> 502. Raise the backend keepalive above the ALB idle timeout so the LB never reuses a closed connection.
<|call:apply_k8s_manifest|> {"kind": "Deployment", "name": "payments-api", "patch": {"env": {"GUNICORN_KEEPALIVE": "75"}}}
System Response: rollout complete; backend keepalive now 75s (> 60s idle); sporadic 502s gone over the next 24h
Resolution: Root cause was the backend keepalive (5s) being shorter than the ALB idle timeout (60s), letting the LB reuse a closed socket and emit 502; fix raised the backend keepalive to 75s above the idle timeout, leaving the LB config unchanged.

Reasoning: Default-backend 404 with healthy pods and a silent controller usually means the controller never adopted this Ingress -- an ingressClass mismatch. Inspect the Ingress's class and status.
<|call:inspect_ingress_config|> {"name": "staging-portal", "fields": "ingressClassName,host,backend,status"}
System Response: ingressClassName=nginx, host=staging.acme.io, backend=staging-portal-svc:80, status.loadBalancer empty (no address)
Recovery reasoning: status.loadBalancer is empty so no controller claimed it; the class 'nginx' looks right, which is the trap. Check whether the internal controller ever even saw the object.
<|call:get_pod_logs|> {"pod_selector": "app.kubernetes.io/name=ingress-nginx-internal", "filter": "staging-portal", "since": "25m"}
System Response: controller log has zero references to staging-portal -- it never observed the Ingress
Recovery reasoning: The controller never saw it, so the class 'nginx' matches no controller in this cluster. List the IngressClasses to find the real one.
<|call:inspect_ingress_config|> {"resource": "ingressclass", "list": true, "fields": "name,controller"}
System Response: classes nginx-internal (k8s.io/ingress-nginx) and nginx-external (k8s.io/ingress-nginx); no class named "nginx"
Recovery reasoning: This is a two-controller cluster and staging.acme.io is served by the internal controller; set ingressClassName to nginx-internal so it gets adopted.
<|call:apply_k8s_manifest|> {"kind": "Ingress", "name": "staging-portal", "patch": {"spec": {"ingressClassName": "nginx-internal"}}}
System Response: ingress adopted by ingress-nginx-internal; status.loadBalancer populated; staging.acme.io now returns 200 from the portal
Resolution: Root cause was an ingressClassName of 'nginx' that matched no IngressClass in a two-controller cluster, so the Ingress went unclaimed; fix set it to nginx-internal, routing it without touching the external controller's traffic.

Reasoning: Same listener, one host gets the right cert and the other gets the default -- that's an SNI miss: the listener has no cert matching admin.acme.io so it falls back to the default. Verify the cert actually served for admin via SNI.
<|call:check_tls_certificate|> {"host": "admin.acme.io", "sni": "admin.acme.io", "port": 443}
System Response: handshake OK, served cert subject CN=api.acme.io SAN=[api.acme.io], valid chain, not expired
Recovery reasoning: The cert is valid and unexpired -- TLS 'works', which is the trap -- it's just the wrong cert because admin isn't covered by any listener cert. List the listener's attached certificates.
<|call:get_listener_rules|> {"load_balancer": "acme-alb", "listener": 443, "fields": "certificates,sni_hosts"}
System Response: default cert api.acme.io; additional certs [grafana.acme.io]; no cert covers admin.acme.io
Recovery reasoning: admin.acme.io has no SNI cert on the listener so ALB serves the default api cert. Attach the admin certificate as an additional listener cert; the default stays as-is.
<|call:attach_listener_certificate|> {"load_balancer": "acme-alb", "listener": 443, "domain": "admin.acme.io", "cert_arn": "arn:aws:acm:us-east-1:111122223333:certificate/9f-admin"}
System Response: certificate added to listener; SNI for admin.acme.io now serves CN=admin.acme.io; browser cert error cleared
Resolution: Root cause was admin.acme.io having no matching SNI certificate on the shared 443 listener, so ALB served the default api.acme.io cert; fix attached the admin cert as an additional listener certificate, leaving the default and other SNI certs intact.

Reasoning: Resets only during pod termination mean in-flight requests are cut because the pod dies before the LB stops sending to it. Check the target group deregistration delay first.
<|call:get_target_group_attributes|> {"target_group": "orders-svc-tg", "fields": "deregistration_delay.timeout_seconds,deregistration_delay.connection_termination.enabled"}
System Response: deregistration_delay.timeout_seconds=300, connection_termination.enabled=false -- looks generously configured
Recovery reasoning: A 300s drain looks fine on paper, which is the trap, so the LB isn't the problem -- the pod must be exiting before deregistration completes. Inspect the pod lifecycle and grace period.
<|call:inspect_k8s_cluster|> {"resource": "deployment", "name": "orders-svc", "fields": "preStop,terminationGracePeriodSeconds,readinessProbe"}
System Response: preStop=none; terminationGracePeriodSeconds=30; the app closes its listener immediately on SIGTERM
Recovery reasoning: With no preStop sleep the pod closes its listener instantly while the ALB is still mid-deregistration, so in-flight requests reset. Add a preStop sleep longer than the deregistration propagation so the pod keeps serving until the LB stops sending.
<|call:apply_k8s_manifest|> {"kind": "Deployment", "name": "orders-svc", "patch": {"lifecycle": {"preStop": {"exec": {"command": ["sh", "-c", "sleep 20"]}}}, "terminationGracePeriodSeconds": 45}}
System Response: rolled out; next deploy showed 0 resets and in-flight requests drained cleanly per pod
Resolution: Root cause was pods closing their listener on SIGTERM with no preStop delay while the ALB was still deregistering, cutting in-flight requests; fix added a 20s preStop sleep and a 45s grace period so each pod serves until the LB finishes draining.

Reasoning: All clients collapsing to one internal IP after putting an LB in front means the app isn't reading the real client IP, so the rate limiter buckets everyone together. Check what header is present versus what the app trusts.
<|call:query_access_logs|> {"service": "edge-api", "fields": "client_ip,x_forwarded_for", "limit": 5}
System Response: client_ip=10.0.3.12 on all rows; x_forwarded_for header IS present and correct, e.g. "203.0.113.7, 10.0.3.12"
Recovery reasoning: The real IP is right there in X-Forwarded-For -- the data exists, which is the trap; the app just isn't using it. The controller must not be rewriting real-ip. Check the controller's forwarded-headers config.
<|call:inspect_nginx_config|> {"scope": "ingress-controller", "fields": "use-forwarded-headers,compute-full-forwarded-for,proxy-real-ip-cidr"}
System Response: use-forwarded-headers=false; proxy-real-ip-cidr=0.0.0.0/0 (default); controller passes the pod IP as remote_addr
Recovery reasoning: use-forwarded-headers is false so the controller ignores XFF and the app sees the pod IP. Enable forwarded-headers and trust the ALB CIDR so remote_addr becomes the real client IP for the limiter.
<|call:apply_k8s_manifest|> {"kind": "ConfigMap", "name": "ingress-nginx-controller", "patch": {"use-forwarded-headers": "true", "proxy-real-ip-cidr": "10.0.0.0/16"}}
System Response: controller reloaded; client_ip now shows 203.0.113.7 etc.; the rate-limit 429 storm cleared
Resolution: Root cause was the ingress controller not honoring X-Forwarded-For after the ALB move, so the limiter saw a single pod IP; fix enabled use-forwarded-headers and trusted the ALB CIDR, restoring per-client IPs without loosening the limiter thresholds.

Reasoning: A fully green target group serving 503s after an image swap is suspicious -- the health check is probably passing on a port the new image still serves while real traffic goes elsewhere. Confirm the green state and compare health vs traffic port.
<|call:check_lb_target_health|> {"target_group": "catalog-svc-tg", "include": "state,health_check_port,port"}
System Response: 8/8 healthy; health_check_port=8080; target traffic port=80 -- all green
Recovery reasoning: There's the trap: health checks hit 8080 (healthy) but the listener forwards real traffic to port 80. The new image may only listen on 8080. Verify which port the container actually serves.
<|call:describe_service_endpoints|> {"service": "catalog-svc", "fields": "container_ports,listening"}
System Response: new image listens ONLY on 8080; port 80 closed -- endpoint :80 connection refused (old image served 80)
Recovery reasoning: The new image moved to 8080 but the target group still forwards traffic to the now-closed 80. Before repointing, rule out a security-group block on 80 so I don't mask a firewall issue.
<|call:check_security_group|> {"target": "catalog-svc-nodes", "port": 80, "source_sg": "catalog-svc-alb-sg"}
System Response: SG allows both 80 and 8080 from the ALB SG -- not a firewall issue; port 80 simply isn't bound by the new container
Recovery reasoning: Purely a port mismatch: health on 8080 stays green while traffic to 80 is refused. Repoint the target group's traffic port to 8080 to match the container.
<|call:modify_lb_attribute|> {"target_group": "catalog-svc-tg", "attributes": {"port": 8080}}
System Response: target port updated to 8080; targets re-register; 503s clear and catalog-svc returns 200
Resolution: Root cause was the new image listening only on 8080 while the target group still forwarded traffic to the now-closed port 80, with health checks on 8080 masking it as green; fix repointed the traffic port to 8080 to match the container.

Reasoning: One AZ hot with fewer targets behind an NLB, all healthy, is the cross-zone signature: an NLB keeps traffic in-zone by default, so 1a's 2 targets absorb a full third of the flows. Check LB health and flow counts.
<|call:get_lb_metrics|> {"load_balancer": "media-transcoder-nlb", "metric": "healthy_host_count,active_flow_count", "window": "15m"}
System Response: healthy_host_count=14/14 across 3 AZs, active_flow_count steady -- LB reports fully healthy, no errors
Recovery reasoning: Healthy is the trap; it says nothing about per-AZ skew. Break flows down per AZ to expose the in-zone concentration.
<|call:get_lb_metrics|> {"load_balancer": "media-transcoder-nlb", "metric": "flows_per_az", "window": "15m"}
System Response: 1a 33% over 2 targets (16.5%/target) | 1b 33% over 6 (5.5%/target) | 1c 33% over 6 -- per-target load 3x higher in 1a
Recovery reasoning: Each AZ gets ~1/3 of flows but 1a has far fewer targets, so per-target load is 3x -- classic disabled cross-zone balancing. Enable cross-zone load balancing so flows spread by target count, not by zone.
<|call:modify_lb_attribute|> {"load_balancer": "media-transcoder-nlb", "attributes": {"load_balancing.cross_zone.enabled": "true"}}
System Response: cross-zone enabled; flows redistributed evenly across all 14 targets; 1a p99 1.8s->0.6s
Resolution: Root cause was cross-zone load balancing disabled on the NLB with uneven targets per AZ, concentrating a third of traffic on 1a's two targets; fix enabled cross-zone balancing so load spreads per target across all AZs.

Reasoning: 404 through the ingress but 200 directly on the pod means the path the ingress forwards doesn't match what the app expects -- a rewrite issue. Reproduce through the ingress and capture the status and who answered.
<|call:curl_endpoint|> {"url": "https://acme.io/api/v2/orders", "method": "GET"}
System Response: HTTP 404 Not Found, server nginx -- body is the orders-svc app's own 404, not the ingress default backend
Recovery reasoning: It reaches orders-svc (app 404, not ingress default), so routing is right but the path upstream is wrong; the app sees /api/v2/orders instead of /orders. Inspect the ingress path and rewrite-target.
<|call:inspect_ingress_config|> {"name": "orders-svc", "fields": "rules.path,annotations.rewrite-target"}
System Response: path=/api/v2(/|$)(.*) pathType=ImplementationSpecific; annotations.rewrite-target = NOT SET
Recovery reasoning: The regex captures the suffix but with no rewrite-target nginx forwards the literal /api/v2/orders, so the app 404s. Set rewrite-target=/$2 to forward only the captured suffix.
<|call:apply_k8s_manifest|> {"kind": "Ingress", "name": "orders-svc", "patch": {"metadata": {"annotations": {"nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/rewrite-target": "/$2"}}}}
System Response: ingress reloaded; upstream now receives "/orders"; GET /api/v2/orders returns 200
Resolution: Root cause was a capture-group path with no rewrite-target, so the ingress forwarded the full /api/v2 prefix and the app 404'd; fix set rewrite-target=/$2 to strip the prefix and forward /orders, leaving other services' routing untouched.

Reasoning: HTTP works but the WS upgrade fails with a clean close means the ingress isn't passing the Upgrade/Connection headers, so the handshake never completes. Reproduce the upgrade and inspect the response.
<|call:curl_endpoint|> {"url": "https://live-feed.acme.io/socket", "headers": {"Connection": "Upgrade", "Upgrade": "websocket"}, "include_response_headers": true}
System Response: HTTP/1.1 200 OK (not 101 Switching Protocols); response lacks Upgrade/Connection headers; connection closed
Recovery reasoning: A 200 instead of 101 looks like success but is wrong -- that's the trap; the backend never saw the upgrade because the ingress stripped it. Check the route's proxy config for upgrade passthrough.
<|call:inspect_nginx_config|> {"scope": "ingress", "name": "live-feed", "fields": "proxy_set_header.Upgrade,proxy_set_header.Connection,proxy_http_version"}
System Response: proxy_http_version=1.0; proxy_set_header Upgrade/Connection absent for this server block
Recovery reasoning: The new ingress proxies HTTP/1.0 with no Upgrade forwarding, so websockets can't switch protocols. Set proxy-http-version 1.1 and enable websocket passthrough for this route only.
<|call:apply_k8s_manifest|> {"kind": "Ingress", "name": "live-feed", "patch": {"metadata": {"annotations": {"nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/proxy-http-version": "1.1", "nginx.org/websocket-services": "live-feed-svc"}}}}
System Response: ingress reloaded; handshake now returns 101 Switching Protocols; wss connections stay open and the 1006 closes are gone
Resolution: Root cause was the migrated ingress proxying HTTP/1.0 without forwarding Upgrade/Connection headers, so the WS handshake never completed; fix set proxy-http-version 1.1 and enabled websocket passthrough for the route, leaving plain HTTP intact.

Reasoning: gRPC failing where REST works on a freshly onboarded service almost always means the target group treats the backend as HTTP/1 instead of HTTP/2. Check the target group protocol version first.
<|call:describe_target_group|> {"target_group": "pricing-grpc-tg", "fields": "protocol,protocol_version,health_check_protocol"}
System Response: protocol=HTTP protocol_version=HTTP1 health_check=HTTP/1.1 expecting 200 -- health checks pass, group healthy
Recovery reasoning: Health checks pass over HTTP/1.1, so the group is green -- the trap -- but a gRPC backend needs HTTP/2 end-to-end; HTTP1 to an h2 backend yields the connect error. Confirm the listener rule and health matcher.
<|call:get_listener_rules|> {"load_balancer": "pricing-alb", "fields": "target_group,health_check_matcher,protocol_version"}
System Response: rule -> pricing-grpc-tg; protocol_version=HTTP1; health_check_matcher expects HTTP 200, not gRPC status
Recovery reasoning: The TG and rule are HTTP1 with an HTTP matcher while the backend speaks gRPC. Switch the target group to GRPC with a gRPC health matcher so the whole path is HTTP/2 gRPC.
<|call:set_target_group_protocol|> {"target_group": "pricing-grpc-tg", "protocol_version": "GRPC", "health_check": {"protocol": "GRPC", "matcher": "0-99"}}
System Response: target group switched to GRPC; health checks pass on gRPC status 0; clients now get OK responses, UNAVAILABLE cleared
Resolution: Root cause was the ALB target group serving the gRPC backend as HTTP/1 with an HTTP health matcher, so HTTP/2 gRPC frames never parsed; fix set the target group protocol_version to GRPC with a gRPC health matcher, making the path HTTP/2 end-to-end.
